<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jayanegara</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1309">1309</a>–<a href="/years/1328">1328</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Jayanagara, Kala Gemet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jayanegara was the second monarch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, ruling from 1309 until his death in 1328, and the only son of its founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raden-wijaya">Raden Wijaya</a>. Accounts differ over his mother: inscriptions name Queen Tribhuwana, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pararaton">Pararaton</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagarakretagama">Nagarakretagama</a> name the Malayu princess Dara Petak. His reign was troubled by rebellions, above all that of Kuti in 1319, which seized the capital until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gajah-mada">Gajah Mada</a>, commander of the palace guard, crushed it and began his rise at court. The Pararaton calls him Kala Gemet, the weak villain. He was killed by his doctor Tanca in 1328, and the true nature of the murder remains unknown.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jayanegara's formal regnal name was Sri Maharaja Wiralandagopala Sri Sundarapandya Dewa Adhiswara, and from 1295 he was Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kediri-kingdom">Kediri</a>. His father, Dyah Wijaya, who took the throne as King Kertarajasa Jayawardhana in about 1293/1294, had married all four daughters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kertanegara">Kertanegara</a> to secure his claim to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singhasari">Singhasari</a> legacy, and also Indreswari, or Dara Petak, a princess of the Malayu Kingdom brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> by Kertanegara's Pamalayu expedition.</p><p>His name comes from the Sanskrit-derived Old Javanese words jaya, glorious, and nagara, city or nation, and so means glorious nation. To reconcile the differing accounts of his mother, some historians suggest that Jayanegara was Dara Petak's son by birth but was adopted and raised by the childless Queen Tribhuwaneswari.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Former companions in arms of his father rose against him, among them Gajah Biru in 1314. During the Kuti rebellion of 1319 the king barely escaped from the capital with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gajah-mada">Gajah Mada</a> and the palace guard and hid in the village of Badander. Gajah Mada returned to the capital, found that Kuti had the support of neither the people nor the nobles, and led the forces that crushed the revolt.</p><p>Tradition remembers the king as strikingly handsome but immoral, desiring the wives and daughters of his subordinates. He kept his half-sisters, Princess Tya and Princess Wiyat, in a fortified palace, unmarried past a suitable age, perhaps to prevent their suitors from contesting his throne. His Malayu mother also made him seem foreign rather than truly Javanese. He sent embassies to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from 1325 to 1328.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gajah-mada">Gajah Mada</a> killed Tanca at once, before any investigation. George Coedes wrote that the king was assassinated by a noble whose wife he had seduced, and some historians identify that noble with Tanca; another theory suspects Gajah Mada, a trusted adviser of Princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tribhuwana-wijayatunggadewi">Tribhuwana Wijayatunggadewi</a>. Jayanegara died childless. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gayatri-rajapatni">Gayatri Rajapatni</a> had become a bhikkhuni, so her daughter Tribhuwana became regent, and Tribhuwana's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayam-wuruk">Hayam Wuruk</a> became king in 1350. The Bajang Ratu gate at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trowulan">Trowulan</a> has traditionally been linked to Jayanegara's memorial compound.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jayanegara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The throne passed within the house of Raden Wijaya, and a woman could hold it: when <b>Jayanegara</b> was murdered in 1328, childless, the Queen Grandmother Gayatri Rajapatni, who had become a Buddhist nun, set her daughter Tribhuwana on it.</blockquote><a href="/societies/majapahit">Social Hierarchy of Majapahit</a></li></ul></section>
